WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.500
String security is a roadmap, not a single safe function call.

2
00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:14.120
Treat strings as structured data with a source, owner, lifetime, length, encoding, validation rule, and output context.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.620
When those properties are implicit, mistakes become much easier to miss.

4
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.620
Prefer std::string for owned text when the application needs to keep data.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:27.800
Use std::string_view only when lifetime is clear.

6
00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:36.860
Do not confuse text with binary data, and do not assume that null termination, byte count, capacity, and displayed character count all mean the same thing.

7
00:00:37.140 --> 00:00:48.260
Validate length, format, allowed characters, encoding, and domain rules before using strings in parsing, policy decisions, file paths, logs, or output.

8
00:00:48.360 --> 00:00:55.880
Normalize before security-sensitive comparison where the application requires canonical text, and document the form that is expected.

9
00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:57.680
Output discipline matters.

10
00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:06.420
Format strings should be controlled by the program, structured output should use context-appropriate escaping, and logs should avoid secrets and sensitive personal data.

11
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:15.080
Sensitive strings need special handling around copying, access, lifetime, cleanup expectations, and redaction.

12
00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:24.000
Finally, isolate legacy APIs behind small wrappers, test boundary cases, use diagnostics, and review string flows deliberately.

13
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:34.660
Secure string handling is ordinary engineering discipline: make assumptions visible, constrain data before trust, and verify behavior at the boundaries where strings matter most.
